      title: 'KUJONG (COUILLON)',
      subtitle: 'Belgian Partnership Trick-Taker',
    },
    sections: [
      {
        type: 'about',
        tabLabel: 'About',
        pages: [
          {
            title: 'Kujong (Couillon)',
            subtitle: 'Belgian Partnership Trick-Taker',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: "Kujong (known in Belgium as Couillon) is a fast-paced trick-taking card game popular in the Low Countries (Belgium, Netherlands) and parts of Germany and Luxembourg. Played by four players in two fixed teams, it uses a 24-card deck. The game is known for its 'erasing' scoring system, where teams start with a 'tree' of 10-12 branches and aim to be the first to prune their tree to zero by winning rounds.",
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: "Dates to the 19th century as a simplified variant of the French Jass and Belote families. The name 'Couillon' is Walloon French slang; in Flemish regions, it is often called 'Kwajongen' or 'Troeven'.",
              },
            ],
          },
          {
            title: 'Setup',
            subtitle: 'Table, deck, equipment, and deal',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: '4 players in two fixed partnerships (A+C vs B+D).',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: '24 cards: A, K, Q, J, 10, 9 in four suits. Ace is highest (4 pts), King (3), Queen (2), Jack (1).',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: '24 cards: A, K, Q, J, 10, 9 in four suits. Ace is highest (4 pts), King (3), Queen (2), Jack (1).',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Each player is dealt 3 cards initially. The next card is turned up for the Trump suit. After the suit is accepted or rejected, each player gets 3 more cards.',
              },
            ],
          },
          {
            title: 'Variations',
            subtitle: 'Known ways this game changes',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'list',
                style: 'unordered',
                items: [
                  {
                    text: 'Couillon: The standard Belgian/French variant.',
                  },
                  {
                    text: 'Kwajongen: The Flemish version with identical rules but different terminology.',
                  },
                  {
                    text: 'Troeven: A simplified variant without the 21-point threshold.',
                  },
                ],
              },
            ],
          },
        ],
      },
